Pulmonary Embolism
Acute Urgent Care
Pleuritic chest pain, Tachypnoea/Tachycardia, Unilateral calf swelling, CTPA filling defect

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-044
Reason for Visit: Sudden, sharp chest pain when taking a deep breath, accompanied by a racing heart and a swollen, painful calf.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who recently returned from a long-haul flight presenting with sudden pleuritic chest pain, shortness of breath, and a visibly swollen right calf. This classic triad strongly suggests a deep vein thrombosis that has embolized to the lungs. Urgent, immediate clinical assessment was necessary to verify signs of right heart strain and coordinate emergency transport for a CT Pulmonary Angiogram, as delaying evaluation risks sudden, fatal right ventricular failure.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every time I take a breath, it feels like a knife in my ribs, and my heart is hammering out of my chest.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with the sudden onset of severe, sharp pleuritic chest pain that began 3 hours ago. The pain is severely exacerbated by deep inspiration and coughing. The patient reports unexplained tachypnoea and a feeling of air hunger. There is a prominent resting tachycardia. The patient also complains of right lower leg pain, noting unilateral calf swelling and tenderness that developed over the last two days following a 14-hour international flight. The patient describes a brief episode of presyncope when walking to the bathroom.
Functional Status: Sitting upright, clutching the chest, taking rapid, shallow breaths.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of massive pulmonary embolism and sudden cardiac arrest.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 118 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 26 | SpO2: 92% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient is anxious, diaphoretic, and in visible respiratory distress, avoiding deep breaths.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals clear lungs on auscultation (no crackles or wheeze), highlighting hypoxaemia out of proportion to clinical lung findings. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates tachycardia. A right ventricular heave is palpable, and there is an accentuated pulmonary component of the second heart sound (loud P2), indicating acute right heart strain. Lower extremity exam reveals significant unilateral swelling, warmth, and deep tenderness in the right calf muscle, highly indicative of a deep vein thrombosis. Expected investigations include a CT pulmonary angiography demonstrating focal intraluminal filling defects in the pulmonary arteries and a highly elevated D-dimer.
Environmental Observations: Suitcases from recent travel are still unpacked in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Does not take oral contraceptives.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pulmonary Embolism - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain being strictly pleuritic (worse with breathing) rather than a constant crushing pressure, and the presence of a swollen calf pointing directly to a venous thromboembolic source.

Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of high fever, purulent sputum, and clear lung fields on auscultation, making an infectious etiology less probable than a vascular occlusion.

Musculoskeletal Chest Wall Pain - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of resting tachycardia, hypoxaemia, and objective signs of right heart strain (loud P2, RV heave), which confirm serious cardiopulmonary pathology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the diagnostic tetrad for a Pulmonary Embolism: sudden pleuritic chest pain, tachypnoea/tachycardia, unilateral calf swelling (DVT source), and strong clinical suspicion requiring CTPA. The presence of presyncope and right heart strain signs (loud P2) indicates a potentially massive, life-threatening embolus. An urgent after-hours home assessment was unequivocally required; identifying this massive vascular blockage and immediately triggering emergency transport for definitive anticoagulation or thrombolysis prevented the patient from progressing to catastrophic right heart failure and death before morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Maintained patient in a seated, upright position to optimize oxygenation. Advised strict bed rest to prevent further embolization of the calf clot. Immediately called 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport to a major Emergency Department equipped for CTPA imaging.
Positive Behavioral Change: By ensuring you stand up, walk around the cabin, and perform calf stretches every two hours during future long-haul flights, you keep the blood flowing vigorously through your leg veins, physically preventing stagnant blood from forming dangerous clots.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics; requires urgent ED and respiratory/hematology review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The spouse must monitor the patient continuously. If the patient loses consciousness or stops breathing entirely, this may indicate a massive saddle embolus. The spouse must lay the patient flat and commence CPR immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Your symptoms and travel history strongly suggest a blood clot has traveled from your leg into your lungs. This requires an urgent specialized CT scan at the hospital.
Clinical Course: The hospital will administer strong blood-thinning medications immediately. If the clot is very large, they may need to give you a medication to actively dissolve it to save your heart.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand up, walk, or rub your sore leg under any circumstances. Any movement could dislodge more of the clot and send it straight to your lungs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A clot in your lung is acting like a dam, forcing your right heart to pump against massive resistance, which is why your heart is racing and you feel faint. You need to be in an ambulance immediately so they can provide oxygen and rush you to the scanner.
Actionable Steps: Try to take slow, shallow breaths to keep the chest pain manageable. Let your spouse do all the talking. We are keeping you completely still while we wait.

